  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Quantification of gallium cryo-FIB milling damage in biological lamella (2023) in EMPIAR dataset. SDG alignment: Zero hunger.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Quantification of gallium cryo-FIB milling damage in biological lamella (2023) in EMPIAR dataset is authored by the authors. Topics: Lamella (surface anatomy), Gallium, Materials science, Nanotechnology and Metallurgy.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">5 citations (OpenAlex). Citing venues include Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Limited online visibility beyond citations.</p>",
  "provenance": {},
  "story_type": "DataStory",
  "publisher": "Unknown",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article titled \"Quantification of gallium cryo-FIB milling damage in biological lamella\" has quickly gained traction within the scientific community, accumulating five citations since its publication in 2023. The research contributes to materials science and nanotechnology, specifically investigating the effects of gallium on biological lamella structures.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This scholarly article explores the implications of gallium cryo-focused ion beam (FIB) milling on biological lamella, a major concern in materials science and nanotechnology. The work addresses critical issues surrounding cryo-FIB processing, focusing on quantifying damage levels to enhance the understanding and application of this technique in biological contexts.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in the open-access EMPIAR dataset, this research makes a significant contribution to sustainability and policy frameworks, particularly aligning with the Sustainable Development Goal of Zero Hunger. By improving material processing techniques, it holds potential implications for food security and agricultural development.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The article has been noted for its high visibility, particularly with its citation in a prominent venue such as the Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences. It appears that the study is beginning to influence ongoing work in the field, as indicated by its early citations and downstream reuse in related articles, underscoring its relevance and integration into the broader scientific discourse.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Furthermore, drawing attention from researchers and institutions worldwide\u2014including the University of Massachusetts Chan Medical School\u2014this study positions itself as a foundation for future research aiming to minimize damage in biological samples subjected to advanced milling techniques. It uniquely bridges the gap between material science and biological applications, fostering interdisciplinary dialogue and further exploration in this evolving field.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
